1. Introduction
  - The Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) dynamically assigns IP addresses to devices.
  - Network administrator do not have to spend your day configuring IP addresses for every device on network.
  - Learn how to configure a Cisco IOS router to be a DHCPv4 server.
  - Learn how to configure a Cisco IOS router as a client.

2. DHCPv4 Concepts
  2.1 DHCPv4 Server and Client  
      - DHCPv4 assigns IPv4 addresses and other network configuration information dynamically.
      - A dedicated DHCPv4 server is scalable and relatively easy to manage.
      - DHCPv4 server dynamically assigns, or leases, an IPv4 address from a pool of addresses for a limited period of time chosen by the server, or until the client no longer needs the address.
      - Clients lease the information from the server for an administratively defined period.
      - Administrators configure DHCPv4 servers to set the leases to time out at different intervals.
      - The lease is typically anywhere from 24 hours to a week or more.
      - When the lease expires, the client must ask for another address, although the client is typically reassigned the same address.
  2.2 DHCPv4 Operation
  2.3 Steps to Obtain a Lease
      a. DHCP Discover (DHCPDISCOVER)
      b. DHCP Offer (DHCPOFFER)
      c. DHCP Request (DHCPREQUEST)
      d. DHCP Acknowledgment (DHCPACK)
  2.4 Steps to Renew a Lease
      a. DHCP Request (DHCPREQUEST)
      b. DHCP Acknowledgment (DHCPACK)

3. Configure a Cisco IOS DHCPv4 Server
    3.1 Steps to Configure a Cisco IOS DHCPv4 Server
        a. Exlude IPv4 addresses
            Router(config)# ip dhcp excluded-address low-address [high-address]
        b. Define a DHCPv4 pool name
            Router(config)# ip dhcp pool pool-name  
            Router(dhcp-config)#
        c. Configure the DHCPv4 pool
            network network-number [mask | /prefix-length]
            default-router address [address2...address8]
            dns-server address [address2...address8]
            domain-name domain
            lease {days [hours [minutes]] | infinite}
            netbios-name-server address [address2...address8]
        d. Configuration Example
            R1(config)# ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.10.1 192.168.10.9
            R1(config)# ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.10.254
            R1(config)# ip dhcp pool LAN-POOL-1
            R1(dhcp-config)# network 192.168.10.0 255.255.255.0
            R1(dhcp-config)# default-router 192.168.10.1
            R1(dhcp-config)# dns-server 192.168.11.5
            R1(dhcp-config)# domain-name example.com
            R1(dhcp-config)# end
            R1#
        e. DHCPv4 Verification Commands
            - show running-config | section dhcp
            - show ip dhcp binding
            - show ip dhcp server statistics
        f. Verify DHCPv4 is Operational
            R1# show running-config | section dhcp
            R1# show ip dhcp binding
            R1# show ip dhcp server statistics
            C:\Users\Student> ipconfig /all
        g. Disable the Cisco IOS DHCPv4 Server
            R1(config)# no service dhcp
            R1(config)# service dhcp
        h. DHCPv4 Relay
            C:\Users\Student> ipconfig /release
            C:\Users\Student> ipconfig /renew
            R1(config)# interface g0/0/0
            R1(config-if)# ip helper-address 192.168.11.6
            R1(config-if)# end
            R1# show ip interface g0/0/0
            C:\Users\Student> ipconfig /all
        i. Other Service Broadcasts Relayed
            - DHCPv4 is not the only service that the router can be configured to relay. 
            - By default, the ip helper-address command forwards the following eight UDP services:
              Port 37: Time
              Port 49: TACACS
              Port 53: DNS
              Port 67: DHCP/BOOTP server
              Port 68: DHCP/BOOTP client
              Port 69: TFTP
              Port 137: NetBIOS name service
              Port 138: NetBIOS datagram service

4. Configure a DHCPv4 Client
    - To configure an Ethernet interface as a DHCP client: ip address dhcp
    - Configuration example
      SOHO(config)# interface G0/0/1
      SOHO(config-if)# ip address dhcp
      SOHO(config-if)# no shutdown
      SOHO# show ip interface g0/0/1 (confirms that the interface is up and that the address was allocated by a DHCPv4 server)
    - Home Router as a DHCPv4 Client
      - Home routers are typically already set to receive IPv4 addressing information automatically from the ISP. 
      - This is so that customers can easily set up the router and connect to the internet.
      - For example, the figure shows the default WAN setup page for a Packet Tracer wireless router. 
      - Notice that the internet connection type is set to Automatic Configuration - DHCP. 
      - This selection is used when the router is connected to a DSL or cable modem and acts as a DHCPv4 client, requesting an IPv4 address from the ISP.
      - Various manufacturers of home routers will have a similar setup.
